\id MRK
\h Mark
\mt Mark
\c 1
\p
\s The Mission of John the Baptist
\v 1 This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
\v 2 As it is written in Isaiah the prophet: “Behold, I will send My messenger ahead of You,who will prepare Your way.” \f + \fr 1:2 \ft Malachi 3:1\f*
\v 3 “A voice of one calling in the wilderness,‘Prepare the way for the Lord, make straight paths for Him.’”
\v 4 John the Baptist appeared in the wilderness, preaching a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins.
\v 5 People went out to him from all of Jerusalem and the countryside of Judea. Confessing their sins, they were baptized by him in the Jordan River.
\v 6 John was clothed in camel’s hair, with a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
\v 7 And he proclaimed: “After me will come One more powerful than I, the straps of whose sandals I am not worthy to stoop down and untie.
\v 8 I baptize you with water,but He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit.”
\v 9 In those days Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan.
\v 10 As soon as Jesus came up out of the water, He sawthe heavens breaking open and the Spirit descending on Him like a dove.
\v 11 And a voice came from heaven: “You are My beloved Son; in You I am well pleased.”
\s The Temptation and Preaching of Jesus
\v 12 At once the Spirit drove Jesus into the wilderness,
\v 13 and He was there for forty days, being tempted by Satan. He was with the wild animals, and the angels ministered to Him.
\v 14 After the arrest of John, Jesus went into Galilee and proclaimed the gospel of God.
\v 15 “The time is fulfilled,” He said, “and the kingdom of God is near. Repent and believe in the gospel!”
\s The First Disciples
\v 16 As Jesus was walking beside the Sea of Galilee, He saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
\v 17 “Come, follow Me,” Jesus said, “and I will make you fishers of men.”
\v 18 And at once they left their nets and followed Him.
\v 19 Going on a little farther, He saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat, mending their nets.
\v 20 Immediately Jesus called them, and they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men and followed Him.
\s Jesus Expels an Unclean Spirit
\v 21 Then Jesus and His companions went to Capernaum, and right away Jesus entered the synagogue on the Sabbath and began to teach.
\v 22 The people were astonished at His teaching, because He taught as one who had authority, and not as the scribes.
\v 23 Suddenly a man with an unclean spirit cried out in the synagogue:
\v 24 “What do You want with us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have You come to destroy us? I know who You are—the Holy One of God!”
\v 25 But Jesus rebuked the spirit. “Be silent!” He said. “Come out of him!”
\v 26 At this, the unclean spirit threw the man into convulsions and came out with a loud shriek.
\v 27 All the people were amazed and began to ask one another, “What is this? A new teaching with authority! He commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey Him!”
\v 28 And the news about Jesus spread quickly through the whole region of Galilee.
\s Jesus Heals at Peter’s House
\v 29 As soon as Jesus and His companions had left the synagogue, they went with James and John to the home of Simon and Andrew.
\v 30 Simon’s mother-in-law was sick in bed with a fever, and they promptly told Jesus about her.
\v 31 So He went to her, took her by the hand, and helped her up. The fever left her, and she began to serve them.
\v 32 That evening, after sunset, people brought to Jesus all the sick and demon-possessed,
\v 33 and the whole town gathered at the door.
\v 34 And He healed many who were ill with various diseases and drove out many demons. But He would not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who He was.
\s Jesus Prays and Preaches
\v 35 Early in the morning, while it was still dark, Jesus got up and went out to a solitary place to pray.
\v 36 Simon and his companions went to look for Him,
\v 37 and when they found Him, they said, “Everyone is looking for You!”
\v 38 But Jesus answered, “Let us go on to the neighboring towns so I can preach there as well, for that is why I have come.”
\v 39 So He went throughout Galilee, preaching in their synagogues and driving out demons.
\s The Leper’s Prayer
\v 40 Then a lepercame to Jesus, begging on his knees: “If You are willing, You can make me clean.”
\v 41 Moved with compassion,Jesus reached out His hand and touched the man. “I am willing,” He said. “Be clean!”
\v 42 And immediately the leprosy left him, and the man was cleansed.
\v 43 Jesus promptly sent him away with a stern warning:
\v 44 “See that you don’t tell anyone. But go, show yourself to the priest and present the offering Moses prescribed for your cleansing, as a testimony to them.”
\v 45 But the man went out and openly began to proclaim and spread the news. Consequently, Jesus could no longer enter a town in plain view, but He stayed out in solitary places. Yet people came to Him from every quarter.
\c 2
\p
\s Jesus Heals a Paralytic
\v 1 A few days later Jesus went back to Capernaum. And when the people heard that He was home,
\v 2 they gathered in such large numbers that there was no more room, not even outside the door, as Jesus spoke the word to them.
\v 3 Then a paralytic was brought to Him, carried by four men.
\v 4 Since they were unable to get to Jesus through the crowd, they uncovered the roof above Him, made an opening, and lowered the paralytic on his mat.
\v 5 When Jesus saw their faith, He said to the paralytic, “Son, your sins are forgiven.”
\v 6 But some of the scribes were sitting there and thinking in their hearts,
\v 7 “Why does this man speak like this? He is blaspheming! Who can forgive sins but God alone?”
\v 8 At once Jesus knew in His spirit that they were thinking this way within themselves. “Why are you thinking these things in your hearts?” He asked.
\v 9 “Which is easier: to say to a paralytic, ‘Your sins are forgiven,’ or to say, ‘Get up, pick up your mat, and walk’?
\v 10 But so that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins...” He said to the paralytic,
\v 11 “I tell you, get up, pick up your mat, and go home.”
\v 12 And immediately the man got up, picked up his mat, and walked out in front of them all. As a result, they were all astounded and glorified God, saying, “We have never seen anything like this!”
\s Jesus Calls Levi
\v 13 Once again Jesus went out beside the sea. All the people came to Him, and He taught them there.
\v 14 As He was walking along, He saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax booth. “Follow Me,” He told him, and Levi got up and followed Him.
\v 15 While Jesus was dining at Levi’s house, many tax collectors and sinners were eating with Him and His disciples—for there were many who followed Him.
\v 16 When the scribes who were Pharisees saw Jesus eating with these people, they asked His disciples, “Why does He eatwith tax collectors and sinners?”
\v 17 On hearing this, Jesus told them, “It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners.”
\s Questions about Fasting
\v 18 Now John’s disciples and the Pharisees were often fasting. So people came to Jesus and asked, “Why don’t Your disciples fast like John’s disciples and those of the Pharisees?”
\v 19 Jesus replied, “How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while He is with them? As long as He is with them, they cannot fast.
\v 20 But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast.
\s The Patches and the Wineskins
\v 21 No one sews a patch of unshrunk cloth on an old garment. If he does, the new piece will pull away from the old, and a worse tear will result.
\v 22 And no one pours new wine into old wineskins. If he does, the wine will burst the skins, and both the wine and the wineskins will be ruined. Instead, new wine is poured into new wineskins.”
\s The Lord of the Sabbath
\v 23 One Sabbath Jesus was passing through the grainfields, and His disciples began to pick the heads of grain as they walked along.
\v 24 So the Pharisees said to Him, “Look, why are they doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath?”
\v 25 Jesus replied, “Have you never read what David did when he and his companions were hungry and in need?
\v 26 During the high priesthood of Abiathar, he entered the house of God and ate the consecrated bread,which was lawful only for the priests. And he gave some to his companions as well.”
\v 27 Then Jesus declared, “The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath.
\v 28 Therefore, the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath.”
\c 3
\p
\s Jesus Heals on the Sabbath
\v 1 Once again Jesus entered the synagogue, and a man with a withered hand was there.
\v 2 In order to accuse Jesus, they were watching to see if He would heal on the Sabbath.
\v 3 Then Jesus said to the man with the withered hand, “Stand up among us.”
\v 4 And He asked them, “Which is lawful on the Sabbath: to do good or to do evil, to save life or to destroy it?” But they were silent.
\v 5 Jesus looked around at them with anger and sorrow at their hardness of heart. Then He said to the man, “Stretch out your hand.” So he stretched it out, and it was restored.
\v 6 At this, the Pharisees went out and began plotting with the Herodians how they might kill Jesus.
\s Jesus Heals the Multitudes
\v 7 So Jesus withdrew with His disciples to the sea, accompanied by a large crowd from Galilee, Judea,
\v 8 Jerusalem, Idumea, the region beyond the Jordan, and the vicinity of Tyre and Sidon. The large crowd came to Him when they heard what great things He was doing.
\v 9 Jesus asked His disciples to have a boat ready for Him so that the crowd would not crush Him.
\v 10 For He had healed so many that all who had diseases were pressing forward to touch Him.
\v 11 And when the unclean spirits saw Him, they fell down before Him and cried out, “You are the Son of God!”
\v 12 But He warned them sternly not to make Him known.
\s The Twelve Apostles
\v 13 Then Jesus went up on the mountain and called for those He wanted, and they came to Him.
\v 14 He appointed twelve of them, whom He designated as apostles,to accompany Him, to be sent out to preach,
\v 15 and to have authorityto drive out demons.
\v 16 These are the twelve He appointed:Simon (whom He named Peter),
\v 17 James son of Zebedee and his brother John (whom He named Boanerges, meaning “Sons of Thunder”),
\v 18 Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James son of Alphaeus, Thaddaeus, Simon the Zealot,
\v 19 and Judas Iscariot, who betrayed Jesus.
\s A House Divided
\v 20 Then Jesus went home,and once again a crowd gathered, so that He and His disciples could not even eat.
\v 21 When His family heard about this, they went out to take custody of Him, saying, “He is out of His mind.”
\v 22 And the scribes who had come down from Jerusalem were saying, “He is possessed by Beelzebul,” and, “By the prince of the demons He drives out demons.”
\v 23 So Jesus called them together and began to speak to them in parables: “How can Satan drive out Satan?
\v 24 If a kingdom is divided against itself, it cannot stand.
\v 25 If a house is divided against itself, it cannot stand.
\v 26 And if Satan is divided and rises against himself, he cannot stand; his end has come.
\v 27 Indeed, no one can enter a strong man’s house to steal his possessions unless he first ties up the strong man. Then he can plunder his house.
\s The Unpardonable Sin
\v 28 Truly I tell you, the sons of men will be forgiven all sins and blasphemies, as many as they utter.
\v 29 But whoever blasphemes against the Holy Spirit will never be forgiven; he is guilty of eternal sin.”
\v 30 Jesus made this statement because they were saying, “He has an unclean spirit.”
\s Jesus’ Mother and Brothers
\v 31 Then Jesus’ mother and brothers came and stood outside. They sent someone in to summon Him,
\v 32 and a crowd was sitting around Him. “Look,” He was told, “Your mother and brothersare outside, asking for You.”
\v 33 But Jesus replied, “Who are My mother and My brothers?”
\v 34 Looking at those seated in a circle around Him, He said, “Here are My mother and My brothers!
\v 35 For whoever does the will of God is My brother and sister and mother.”
